" (https://homepages.cwi.nl/~rdewolf/publ/qc/matrixbeckner.pdf)\nciteturn1007view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 17; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://homepages.cwi.nl/~rdewolf/publ/qc/matrixbeckner.pdf\",\"lineno\":22}); Total lines: 1539\nL0@P0: A Hypercontractive Inequality for Matrix-Valued Functions\nL1@P0: with Applications to Quantum Computing\nL2@P0: Avraham Ben-Aroya∗ Oded Regev† Ronald de Wolf‡\nL3@P0: Abstract\nL4@P0: The Bonami-Beckner hypercontractive inequality is a powerful tool in Fourier analysis of real-valued\nL5@P0: functions on the Boolean cube. In this paper we present a version of this inequality for matrix-valued\nL6@P0: functions on the Boolean cube. Its proof is based on a powerful inequality by Ball, Carlen, and Lieb. We\nL7@P0: also present a number of applications of this inequality. In particular, we analyze maps that encode n\nL8@P0: classical bits into m qubits, in such a way that each set of k bits can be recovered with some probability\nL9@P0: by an appropriate measurement on the quantum encoding; we show that if m < 0.7n, then the success\nL10@P0: probability is exponentially small in k. This result may be viewed as a direct product version of Nayak’s\nL11@P0: quantum random access code bound. It in turn implies strong direct product theorems for the one-way\nL12@P0: quantum communication complexity of Disjointness and other problems. We also slightly strengthen\nL13@P0: and simplify a result about 3-party communication complexity of Disjointness due to Beame et al.\nL14@P0: ∗Department of Computer Science, Tel-Aviv University, Tel-Aviv 69978, Israel. Supported by the Israel Science Foundation,\nL15@P0: and by the European Commission under the Integrated Project QAP funded by the IST directorate as Contract Number 015848.\nL16@P0: †Department of Computer Science, Tel-Aviv University, Tel-Aviv 69978, Israel. Supported by an Alon Fellowship, by the\nL17@P0: Binational Science Foundation, by the Israel Science Foundation, and by the European Commission under the Integrated Project\nL18@P0: QAP funded by the IST directorate as Contract Number 015848.\nL19@P0: ‡Centrum voor Wiskunde en Informatica (CWI), Amsterdam, The Netherlands. Supported by a Veni grant from the Netherlands\nL20@P0: Organization for Scientific Research (NWO) and also partially supported by the European Commission under the Integrated Project\nL21@P0-1: QAP funded by the IST directorate as Contract Number 015848.1 Introduction\nL22@P1: 1.1 A hypercontractive inequality for matrix-valued functions\nL23@P1: Fourier analysis of real-valued functions on the Boolean cube has been widely used in the theory of comput\u0002ing. Applications include analyzing the influence of variables on Boolean functions [25], probabilistically\u0002checkable proofs [20], analysis of threshold phenomena in random graphs [16], analyzing noise [37], learn\u0002ing under the uniform distribution [30, 31, 23, 32], communication complexity [39, 26, 17], etc.\nL24@P1: One of the main technical tools in this area is a hypercontractive inequality that is sometimes called the\nL25@P1: Bonami-Beckner inequality [9, 7], though its history would also justify other names (see Lecture 16 of [38]\nL26@P1: for some background and history). For a fixed ρ ∈ [0, 1], consider the linear operator Tρ on the space of all\nL27@P1: functions f : {0, 1}\nL28@P1: n → R defined by\nL29@P1: (Tρ(f))(x) = Ey[f(y)]\nL30@P1: where y is obtained from x by negating each bit independently with probability (1 − ρ)/2. In other words,\nL31@P1: the value of Tρ(f) at a point x is obtained by averaging the values of f over a certain neighborhood of x.\nL32@P1: One important property of Tρ for ρ < 1 is that it has a “smoothing” effect: any “high peaks” present in f\nL33@P1: are smoothed out in Tρ(f). The hypercontractive inequality formalizes this intuition. To state it precisely,\nL34@P1: define the p-norm of a function f by kfkp = ( 1\nL35@P1: 2n\nL36@P1: P\nL37@P1: x\nL38@P1: |f(x)|\nL39@P1: p\nL40@P1: )\nL41@P1: 1/p. It is not difficult to prove that the norm\nL42@P1: is nondecreasing with p. Also, the higher p is, the more sensitive the norm becomes to peaks in the function\nL43@P1: f. The hypercontractive inequality says that for certain q > p, the q-norm of Tρ(f) is upper bounded by the\nL44@P1: p-norm of f. This exactly captures the intuition that Tρ(f) is a smoothed version of f: even though we are\nL45@P1: considering a higher norm, the norm does not increase. More precisely, the hypercontractive inequality says\nL46@P1: that as long as 1 ≤ p ≤ q and ρ ≤\nL47@P1: p\nL48@P1: (p − 1)/(q − 1), we have\nL49@P1: kTρ(f)kq ≤ kfkp. (1)\nL50@P1: The most interesting case for us is when q = 2, since in this case one can view the inequality as a\nL51@P1: statement about the Fourier coefficients of f, as we describe next. Let us first recall some basic definitions\nL52@P1: from Fourier analysis. For every S ⊆ [n] (which by some abuse of notation we will also view as an n-bit\nL53@P1: string) and x ∈ {0, 1}\nL54@P1: n\nL55@P1: , define χS(x) = (−1)x·Sto be the parity of the bits of x indexed by S. The Fourier\nL56@P1: transform of a function f : {0, 1}\nL57@P1: n → R is the function fb: {0, 1}n → R defined by\nL58@P1: fb(S) = 1\nL59@P1: 2\nL60@P1: n\nL61@P1: X\nL62@P1: x∈{0,1}n\nL63@P1: f(x)χS(x).\nL64@P1: The values fb(S) are called the Fourier coefficients of f. The coefficient fb(S) may be viewed as measuring\nL65@P1: the correlation between f and the parity function χS. Since the functions χS form an orthonormal basis of\nL66@P1: the space of all functions from {0, 1}\nL67@P1: n\nL68@P1: to R, we can express f in terms of its Fourier coefficients as\nL69@P1: f =\nL70@P1: X\nL71@P1: S⊆[n]\nL72@P1: fb(S)χS. (2)\nL73@P1: Using the same reasoning we obtain Parseval’s identity, kfk2 =\nL74@P1: \u0010P\nL75@P1: S⊆[n]\nL76@P1: fb(S)\nL77@P1: 2\nL78@P1: \u00111/2\nL79@P1: .\nL80@P1: The operator Tρ has a part